<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Apelles</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">4th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Apelles was the most celebrated painter of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, active in the 4th century BCE. Born at Colophon, he trained at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a> and in the painting school of Sicyon and became court painter to Philip II and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>. None of his works survive, but ancient writers praised his Aphrodite rising from the sea and his portraits of Alexander.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/apelles.jpg" alt="Apelles" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Giovanni Antonio Guardi · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alexander_and_Campaspe_in_Apelles%27_studio_(c.1745),_by_Giovanni_Antonio_Guardi_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
